WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.040
So there's not a direct link, clearly,
between the geology and

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.269
people's votes. But there is this
wonderful chain of cause and effect

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:13.830
through hundreds of years of human
history and then millions of years of

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:19.170
our planet's history. And what was
happening on the planet 75 to 80 million

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.130
years ago in a chapter of Earth's
history called the Cretaceous is the sea

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:29.250
levels were much much higher back then.
The ocean lapped right up through the

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.690
middle of North America and a great
interior sea, and laid down what was

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:39.480
essentially thick deposits of seafloor
mud, which became compacted down, buried

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.760
by more recent strata of rocks, and
then eroded away again and exposed along

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:50.190
this particular arc in the Southern
States. To the north, the rocks are older, to

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:54.690
the south, they're younger. These are the
Cretaceous age rocks. And when that

00:00:54.690 --> 00:01:02.160
rock erodes to create soil, it gives you
a very rich, fertile, dark soil. And it's

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.180
realized in the early 1800s that that
soil is phenomenally productive at

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.650
growing cash crops like cotton—which, as we've already mentioned, unfortunately, in this

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:16.049
period of history, meant slave labor.
Cotton is incredibly finicky to harvest

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:20.640
it's not like cotton or rice where you can
simply chop it off at the stalk take it

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.479
out of your field and then shake a bit
to get the grain that you want. Cotton

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:30.600
requires fingers. And even today after
hundreds of years, after the Civil War

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.009
and emancipation and freedom from
slavery in the Civil Rights Movement,

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:39.270
even today is that Cretaceous band of
rocks that has the highest density of

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:44.310
black African Americans living there
today. People unfortunately still suffer,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:49.649
still are blighted with social economic
problems of poor wages, poor health care,

00:01:49.649 --> 00:01:52.950
poor social structures, people that are
therefore more likely to vote for

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:59.729
Democrat election promises rather than
Republican. And in fact, the city I've

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:05.159
marked here, Montgomery, is where in 1955
Rosa Parks refused to give up her seat

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:10.500
to white gentlemen on the bus. The very
epicenter of the entire Civil Rights

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.950
Movement that transformed society and
politics in

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:19.319
the whole of the United States began
smack in the middle of rocks that are 80

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:22.879
million years old on the surface.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.959
 

